Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.170 align:start position:0%
 
you  arthur c clarke author of 2001 a space  odyssey once said two possibilities  exist either we are alone in the  universe or we are not both are equally  terrifying most media exploring the  topic of alien life in the universe  often work from the assumption that we  are not alone and that our technology is  not sufficiently advanced to detect our  distant neighbors or that they know  we're here and don't think we're worth  contacting or any number of other  explanations  while these hypotheses may turn out to  be true the other possibility that  doesn't get as much attention is that we  are in fact alone in the universe if  that were the case how would it change  our lives and our goals here on earth  for this thought experiment we're going  to assume that humanity has a way to  accurately determine that we are the  only life forms in the universe and work  from there if one day the news broke  that the universe is indeed a cold dead  place and that all life is contained  here on earth it would instantly bring  to mind two possibilities either we are  the very first life in the universe or  the very last and knowing humanity's  affinity for giant explosives we could  very easily be the first and last life  in the universe if we weren't careful  besides our new terrifying realization  that we are one planet away from a  completely sterile universe what would  change let's start with the world's  space programs if we were alone in the  universe we could scrap any missions  designed to search for extraterrestrial  life that would include numerous probes  such as those intended for the icy  landscapes of Europa and Enceladus as  well as entire branches of national  space programs like the SETI Institute  we could use the money from those  programs to redouble our efforts in  preserving the earth and its natural  resources or to fund new schools  hospitals or humanitarian organizations  of course that's probably wishful  thinking but the possibility is there if  we wanted to keep that money in the  space programs we could put it towards  research and development of ways to  ensure humanity's continued survival in  the event of disaster on earth colonies  on the moon Venus Mars or even Prototype  generational ships that could support  human colonies over several lifetimes  would be worthwhile endeavors of course  with an announcement of such magnitude  it would no doubt create polarized  groups within our global society as the  only intelligent life in the universe  would it be our responsive  to seed life on other planets by sending  probes full of bacteria and simple  organisms or are we no more than a  blight on the universe that should be  contained to earth given our history of  colonization on earth it would stand to  reason that we would at least want to  establish colonies as far away from  Earth as possible if only to ensure the  survival of our species in the event of  a gamma-ray burst or some other  Cataclysm but seeding life on other  planets will likely never visit would  probably be met with controversy so far  we've been working under the assumption  that we are the only life in the  universe at all but what if we specified  that we were the only intelligent life  how would that change the scenario well  if we assume again that humanity has a  way to determine that there is no  intelligent life in the universe then  not much will change initially however  imagine a scenario in which a probe from  Earth discovers single-celled life on  another planet let's say Europa in this  scenario we would be the billions of  years more advanced alien species  keeping an eye on new life in the  universe something that many people have  suspected extraterrestrials have been  doing to us over the history of life on  Earth but what would we do about it  to what extent would we interfere with  the growth of a new presence in the  universe many would argue that the best  course of action would be to observe  from afar and allow nature to run its  course no matter the outcome others  might want to watch but take it one step  further and intervene if the new life is  headed for catastrophe then there will  most likely be a portion of society that  thinks we should physically visit Europa  and try to guide the life-forms to  success a dangerous proposition as  humans would carry microbes with them  that could endanger the very life we're  trying to protect as one final  possibility what if we were to discover  the presence of a very primitive species  just starting to use simple tools this  would be the truest test of humanity's  disposition towards extraterrestrial  life as with the previous scenario we  could look to the zoo hypothesis for one  example of how best to handle fledgling  life-forms the zoo hypothesis suggests  that intelligent civilizations would  refrain from visiting planets with life  to allow natural evolution and  socio-cultural development it certainly  seems like the most respectful way to  handle the situation and it's not too  much of a stretch of the imagination to  think that most world leaders could  agree to contact these extraterrestrials  only once they had reached a certain  technological or societal Thresh  of course there would be others who  would want to visit them immediately and  I don't know build pyramids or something  while this may not seem like an  important topic to discuss it allows us  to explore several interesting issues  how would our space programs change if  we were the only life in the universe  would we scrap them entirely and just  focus on our existence here on earth or  would we reallocate those funds to focus  on something more like an escape plan  what a newfound certainty of uniqueness  in the universe prompt us to become a  more peaceful species or would life go  on the way it always has if we  discovered we were not completely alone  but at the top of the totem pole of  intelligence would we act like the  curators of an intergalactic Zoo would  we play the role of a benevolent god  race intervening to prevent disaster  guiding life towards civilization or  would we do the unthinkable and ensure  that no life-forms ever become advanced  enough to threaten our position as the  apex predator of the cosmos these are  only a handful of the questions this  topic can raise and who knows if we'll  ever be able to answer them only time  will tell  leave your thoughts and opinions in the  comments below hopefully we can spark  some interesting conversations and maybe  explore those ideas in future videos if  you enjoyed this video check out this  playlist of all my other space related  content or if you're really bored you  can watch all my videos by clicking here  make sure to subscribe if you haven't  already follow second thought on your  favorite social media and leave a  comment down below with your opinions on  today's thought experiment thanks for  watching and I'll see you in the next  video

